(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-heart-failure-vignettes"]={"title":"Heart Failure Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic left-sided heart failure now has right-sided failure as well. Which management approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Start a calcium channel blocker","Start home inotropic support","Treat as left-sided failure and its cause","Stop his left-sided heart failure drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure is treated the same as left-sided failure, with symptom management and treatment of the underlying cause, moving to transplant or palliative care as it advances. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has grade II diastolic dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 46%. She has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion but has never had pulmonary or peripheral edema and has never needed a diuretic. Does she meet a clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure?","choices":["Yes, because she is older than 65","No, because congestion is required","Yes, because she has symptoms","Yes, because her fraction is 46%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure requires congestion, meaning pulmonary or peripheral edema. She has symptoms and a mildly reduced ejection fraction, but without edema the congestive label does not apply. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction asks why his clinician raises one medicine at a time rather than starting everything at full strength. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Therapy is optimized as tolerated","Full doses are unsafe in any patient","Only one drug can be taken at once","Medicines stop once symptoms go"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed therapy is prioritized for his comorbidities and optimized as hemodynamically stable, so each class is increased as blood pressure and tolerance allow. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, breathlessness with exertion and no symptoms at rest. He takes furosemide and sacubitril-valsartan. Which drug class should be added as core therapy?","choices":["Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem","Milrinone as an outpatient infusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reduced ejection fraction carries strong recommendations for a beta blocker, renin-angiotensin blockade, a mineralocorticoid antagonist and a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor; he has only the renin-angiotensin piece so far. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 45%, and her fraction has never been lower. Which heart failure group describes her?","choices":["Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. 45% falls in the 41 to 49% band, and with no earlier value of 40% or less there is nothing to make it improved ejection fraction. Her treatment then resembles preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is comfortable at rest but becomes breathless after only a few steps across a room. Which New York Heart Association class describes him?","choices":["Class III","Class II","Class IV","Class I"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with only slight exertion, such as a few steps across a room, and none at rest are class III. Class II is symptoms with ordinary exertion and class IV is symptoms at rest. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with preserved ejection fraction heart failure (fraction 58%) is euvolemic on a loop diuretic. He has no diabetes. Which drug class is the next best-supported addition?","choices":["Angiotensin II receptor blocker alone","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In preserved ejection fraction, after diuretics as needed, the sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor carries a moderate recommendation, stronger than the weak ones for the neprilysin combination, antagonist and receptor blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 27%) has blood pressure above goal on her heart failure regimen. Verapamil is proposed. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Add it once she reaches class IV","Avoid it and use recommended classes","Start it as first-line pressure control","Use it in place of her beta blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. Pressure control comes from optimizing the recommended classes as tolerated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, obesity and a normal echocardiogram has no symptoms. His clinician plans to start metoprolol to prevent heart failure. Which drug class should not be chosen first here?","choices":["A sodium-glucose drug such as empagliflozin","Lifestyle risk factor control","Glucose-lowering therapy in general","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He is stage A, at risk with a normal heart, and the first drug for an at-risk patient with diabetes is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has a B-type natriuretic peptide that has risen by 100 since her last visit. What does this change imply?","choices":["About 5% higher relative risk of death","About 90% higher relative risk of death","No change in her relative risk of death","About 35% higher relative risk of death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each increase of 100 carries about a 35% increase in the relative risk of death, so a rising level is a warning sign even when symptoms seem stable. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with known dilated cardiomyopathy has worsening breathlessness. Blood pressure is 102/64, a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) is present, and he has crackles. Which ejection fraction is the echocardiogram expected to show?","choices":["About 45%","About 75%","About 25%","About 60%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is the systolic chain: poor contraction, a reduced ejection fraction of 40% or less, low-normal pressure, a third heart sound and pulmonary edema. A value near 25% fits. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction one week ago with acute heart failure. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 30%. He is now stable. Which is the most appropriate next step for protection against sudden death?","choices":["Home inotropes until the heart recovers","Wearable defibrillator while therapy is optimized","Implantable defibrillator before discharge","No protection until a repeat echo in a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure from infarction with an ejection fraction below 35%, the pathway is a wearable defibrillator vest, optimized guideline-directed therapy with cardiac rehabilitation, a repeat echocardiogram, and an implantable device only if there is no improvement. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman takes sacubitril-valsartan for reduced ejection fraction. Which natriuretic peptide result should not be relied on to judge her congestion?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide","N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide","Either peptide, since both are altered","Neither peptide, since neither is altered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neprilysin degrades B-type natriuretic peptide, so blocking neprilysin with sacubitril alters that level. The N-terminal fragment is the one to rely on. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance and palpitations for two months, and now ankle edema and breathlessness. Heart rate is 124 and B-type natriuretic peptide is elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Right-sided failure from mitral stenosis","High-output failure from hyperthyroidism","Systolic failure from ischemic disease","Diastolic failure from hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyperthyroidism raises peripheral demand, and the heart answers with a faster rate and stronger contraction until the heart failure syndrome appears: fatigue, dyspnea, edema and palpitations. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has no cardiac symptoms. Her echocardiogram is normal. Which drug class is recommended first to lower her risk of heart failure?","choices":["Beta blocker as the first agent","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Loop diuretic such as bumetanide","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors with a normal heart and no symptoms are stage A, at risk for heart failure, and the one strong drug recommendation there is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes, not a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has leg edema, crackles and breathlessness climbing stairs. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 62% and B-type natriuretic peptide is 640. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation now?","choices":["An inotrope such as dobutamine","A diuretic as needed for congestion","A beta blocker titrated to heart rate","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With preserved ejection fraction and congestion, diuretics as needed carry the strong recommendation. A sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is next at moderate strength; the neprilysin combination, antagonist and receptor blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is recovering from acute heart failure after an infarction with an ejection fraction of 29%. He wears a defibrillator vest while his guideline-directed therapy is being optimized. Which referral should accompany this phase?","choices":["Hospice enrollment","Transplant evaluation","Valve surgery review","Cardiac rehabilitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure with an ejection fraction below 35%, the pathway pairs optimized guideline-directed therapy with cardiac rehabilitation while he wears the vest, before the echocardiogram is repeated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man presents for the first time with breathlessness, leg edema, crackles and a B-type natriuretic peptide of 720. Which is the most appropriate next step to classify his heart failure?","choices":["Echocardiogram","Right heart catheterization","Repeat the peptide next week","Exercise stress testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Signs, symptoms and an elevated peptide support a clinical diagnosis, and the echocardiogram then shows structure most accurately and sorts him into an ejection fraction group, which decides therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has breathlessness on exertion for a week. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 45. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Look for a non-cardiac cause","Start a loop diuretic today","Admit for acute heart failure","Order an implantable device"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A level below 100 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, so her breathlessness needs another explanation. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with heart failure asks why she must take several medicines when she already feels better. Which explanation matches the goals of treatment?","choices":["They are meant to cure her cardiomyopathy","They are meant to make her ejection fraction normal","They also extend life and slow progression","They are only needed while she has symptoms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The goals are to decrease symptoms, increase survival and limit disease progression. Feeling better meets only the first of the three, so the regimen continues. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman in stage B, pre-heart failure, developed side effects on lisinopril and could not tolerate it. Which is the recommended alternative?","choices":["Sacubitril-valsartan combination","Verapamil for blood pressure","Losartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker","Stopping renin-angiotensin therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage B lists an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker if the inhibitor is not tolerated, alongside a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with heart failure has cool, clammy extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and sleepiness, along with orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg edema. Which bedside hemodynamic profile fits?","choices":["Cold and wet","Warm and wet","Cold and dry","Warm and dry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-perfusion signs (cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure, obtundation) plus congestion signs (orthopnea, edema) place her in the cold and wet profile, hypoperfusion with congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is newly diagnosed with heart failure and will start a loop diuretic. Which laboratory panel should be checked before starting?","choices":["Thyroid function panel","Hepatic function panel","Coagulation panel","Complete metabolic panel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The metabolic panel gives potassium and renal function (blood urea nitrogen, creatinine and filtration rate), both of which a potassium-wasting loop diuretic affects. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has right-sided heart failure confirmed by echocardiogram. Her team now wants to know how hemodynamically severe it is. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph series","Troponin trend over a day","Right heart catheterization","Repeat echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once right-sided failure is established, right heart catheterization measures its hemodynamic severity. The echocardiogram has already made the diagnosis. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) has gained weight with leg edema and crackles. She takes a beta blocker, sacubitril-valsartan, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which drug relieves her fluid overload now?","choices":["Dobutamine given at home","Hydralazine given on its own","A loop diuretic such as furosemide","Diltiazem to slow the heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are used as needed for congestion in every ejection fraction group; loops such as furosemide and bumetanide are the potassium-wasting diuretics that clear fluid. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure and congestion is started on furosemide. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Potassium and kidney labs will be checked","His liver enzymes will be checked weekly","It will raise his potassium to high levels","No blood tests are needed on this drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide is a potassium-wasting loop diuretic, so potassium and renal function are followed on the complete metabolic panel. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has two days of breathlessness after a viral illness. His N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 90. How should the result be read?","choices":["Heart failure is likely","Acute heart failure is likely","The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is ruled out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An N-terminal fragment below 300 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, and 90 is also below the under-75 heart failure threshold of 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a myocardial infarction three years ago has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). Blood pressure is 104/68. There is a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) and bibasilar crackles. B-type natriuretic peptide is 820. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease","High-output failure from increased demand","Isolated right-sided failure from the lungs","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every link of the systolic chain is here: ischemic cardiomyopathy after infarction, a low-normal blood pressure, a third heart sound from rapid filling of a dilated ventricle, and pulmonary edema, confirmed by an elevated natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has combined right and left heart failure with symptoms at rest despite optimized guideline-directed therapy. Which course is most appropriate to discuss?","choices":["Continuing dobutamine at home","Stopping all medicines and observing","Transplant evaluation or comfort care","Adding a calcium channel blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Combined right and left failure is managed with symptom control, comfort care and heart transplant. When optimized therapy no longer controls symptoms, those are the options to raise. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with heart failure has cool extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and increasing confusion. His urine output has fallen and creatinine has doubled. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Acute kidney injury from low perfusion","Nephrotic syndrome from proteinuria","Urinary retention from prostatic disease","Hypertensive kidney damage from years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure and obtundation are signs of low perfusion, and renal dysfunction with acute kidney injury and falling urine output is part of the same picture. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
